<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Achieving trustworthy Human-Agent-GUI interaction remains a non-trivial challenge.
Based on the analysis above, we identify two primary factors contributing to this difficulty for OS agents: (i) current OS agents are unable to accurately detect untrustworthy scenarios and (ii) even when provided with entirely correct query–answer histories, their performance degrades in otherwise normal task settings.
These observations highlight the need for an OS agent capable of accurately recognizing the current task context, generating appropriate queries to the user, and effectively leveraging user responses to improve task execution. This motivates the design of VeriOS-Agent, which we introduce in the next section.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>VeriOS-Agent</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first introduce how we train VeriOS-Agent using a two-stage learning paradigm that facilitates the decoupling and utilization of meta-knowledge, and then we explain how to use VeriOS-Agent for the proactive query-driven human-agent-GUI interaction.
Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1. Framework Overview ‣ 4. VeriOS-Agent ‣ VeriOS: Query-Driven Proactive Human-Agent-GUI Interaction for Trustworthy OS Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the overall framework.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Framework Overview</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In meta-knowledge decoupling stage, each data instance <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in VeriOS-Bench <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a tuple:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=(P,i,a_{g},e,q,h)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=(P,i,a_{g},e,q,h),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the system prompt, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the user instruction, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{g}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{g}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ground truth action, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the scenario type, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the standard ask query, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the standard human answer. Note that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=h=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=h=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a normal scenario.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each instance encapsulates two distinct forms of knowledge: (i) scenario knowledge for identifying untrustworthy scenarios and generating corresponding asks, denoted as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{\text{scenario}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mtext>scenario</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{\text{scenario}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (ii) action knowledge for generating executable actions using query-answer pairs, denoted as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{\text{action}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mtext>action</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{\text{action}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To enable the OS agent to learn both types of knowledge effectively, we propose a two-stage learning paradigm that facilitates the decoupling and utilization of meta-knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For any instance <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we decouple it into two derived sub-instances: <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The first sub-instance, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, is designed to elicit <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{\text{scenario}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mtext>scenario</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{\text{scenario}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is constructed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}:\text{Input}=\langle P,i\rangle,\quad\text{Output}=\langle e,q\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mtext>Input</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>Output</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}:\text{Input}=\langle P,i\rangle,\quad\text{Output}=\langle e,q\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The second sub-instance, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, is designed to elicit <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{\text{action}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mtext>action</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{\text{action}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is constructed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}:\text{Input}=\langle P,i,q,h\rangle,\quad\text{Output}=\langle e,a_{g}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mtext>Input</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>Output</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}:\text{Input}=\langle P,i,q,h\rangle,\quad\text{Output}=\langle e,a_{g}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The training dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> is then constructed by interleaving the sub-instances from each original instance in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, for each <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we include <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> followed by <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring that the two types of knowledge are learned in an interleaved manner. Formally, if the original dataset is ordered as <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{(1)},d^{(2)},\ldots,d^{(N)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{(1)},d^{(2)},\ldots,d^{(N)}</annotation></semantics></math>, then:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}=\left(d_{1}^{(1)},d_{2}^{(1)},d_{1}^{(2)},d_{2}^{(2)},\ldots,d_{1}^{(N)},d_{2}^{(N)}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}=\left(d_{1}^{(1)},d_{2}^{(1)},d_{1}^{(2)},d_{2}^{(2)},\ldots,d_{1}^{(N)},d_{2}^{(N)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07553v1/figures/method.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/224;" width="476" height="224" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Diagram of the two-stage learning paradigm and query-driven human-agent-GUI interaction. The two-stage learning paradigm consists of the meta-knowledge decoupling stage and the meta-knowledge utilization stage. We first decouple the knowledge from VeriOS-Bench into scenario knowledge and action knowledge, and then leverage this knowledge to construct VeriOS-Agent. During the interaction process, when VeriOS-Agent identifies the current scenario as untrustworthy, it issues a query to the human and utilizes the history of queries and human responses to better accomplish the task.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In meta-knowledge utilization stage, interleaved SFT is performed over <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The training objective for a sub-instance <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with target token sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(1)}=\langle e,q\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(1)}=\langle e,q\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{d_{1}}=-\sum_{t=1}^{L_{1}}\log p\left(y_{t}^{(1)}\mid P,i,y_{&lt;t}^{(1)};\theta\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{d_{1}}=-\sum_{t=1}^{L_{1}}\log p\left(y_{t}^{(1)}\mid P,i,y_{&lt;t}^{(1)};\theta\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> represents the model parameters. Similarly, for a sub-instance <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with target sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(2)}=\langle e,a_{g}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(2)}=\langle e,a_{g}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, the loss is:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{d_{2}}=-\sum_{t=1}^{L_{2}}\log p\left(y_{t}^{(2)}\mid P,i,q,h,y_{&lt;t}^{(2)};\theta\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></munderover><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{d_{2}}=-\sum_{t=1}^{L_{2}}\log p\left(y_{t}^{(2)}\mid P,i,q,h,y_{&lt;t}^{(2)};\theta\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The overall training objective is the average loss over all sub-instances:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}(\theta)=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{1}|}\sum_{d_{1}\in\mathcal{D}_{1}}\mathcal{L}_{d_{1}}+\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{2}|}\sum_{d_{2}\in\mathcal{D}_{2}}\mathcal{L}_{d_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{SFT}}(\theta)=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{1}|}\sum_{d_{1}\in\mathcal{D}_{1}}\mathcal{L}_{d_{1}}+\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{2}|}\sum_{d_{2}\in\mathcal{D}_{2}}\mathcal{L}_{d_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">This framework enables the model to decouple and internalize the two types of meta-knowledge within a unified training process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Query-Driven Human-Agent-GUI Interaction with Temporal Dynamics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When a human provides an instruction <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to the VeriOS-Agent <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> at time step <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> first captures a screenshot <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of the current interface. VeriOS-Agent then determines the type of the current scenario and generates an action:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t},a_{t}^{(0)}=M(i,s_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t},a_{t}^{(0)}=M(i,s_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the judgment of the scenario type at time <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the initially output action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If the scenario type <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is normal, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> must not be an ASK action but rather an action that can directly operate the GUI. In this case, the final executed action is <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}=a_{t}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}=a_{t}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the scenario type <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is one of untrustworthy scenarios, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> must be the ASK action, prompting the VeriOS-Agent to pose a clarifying query to the human.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, the human provides a response <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to the query raised by <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
VeriOS-Agent then performs another round of reasoning using both the query <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and the human response <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t},a_{t}^{(1)}=M(i,s_{t},a_{t}^{(0)},h_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t},a_{t}^{(1)}=M(i,s_{t},a_{t}^{(0)},h_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To prevent the agent from repeatedly asking queries, <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> must not be an ASK action when <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-empty.
The final action executed at time step <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is then <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}=a_{t}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}=a_{t}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
After applying <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to the environment, the state transitions to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The task terminates if either the objective is completed or the maximum number of steps <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> set by the human is reached. Otherwise, the process repeats for time step <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first introduce the experimental setup, followed by main results on VeriOS-Bench.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experiments Setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span>Baselines</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We select a total of 13 popular OS agents as baselines, including those built on open-source models and those based on proprietary models.
The open-source model-based agents include the UI-TARS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> series, Qwen2.5-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025</a>)</cite> series, OS-Atlas-Pro-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025e</a>)</cite>, TARS-1.5-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Seed, 2025</a>)</cite>, and GUI-owl-32B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
The proprietary model-based agents include GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hurst et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Qwen-VL-max <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and GLM-4.5V <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Since GPT-4o inherently lack the capability to predict coordinates, we incorporate an OCR model composed of ResNet18 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2016</a>)</cite> and ConvNeXt-Tiny <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to assist them in localization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2. </span>Metrics</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the experiments of this paper, we evaluate whether the output actions of the OS agent match the ground truth and report the step-wise success rate under different scenarios, as well as the overall step-wise success rate. Here, MC refers to multiple choices, IM refers to information missing, EA refers to environment anomalies, SA refers to sensitive actions, and NS refers to normal scenarios. We also report the scenario judgment accuracy (SJA) to measure the accuracy of the OS agent’s scenario judgment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.3. </span>Implementation Details</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During the training process, we performed full fine-tuning on both the visual and LLM components. The VeriOS-Agent-7B model was trained with a learning rate of 5e-6 for 2 epochs, while the VeriOS-Agent-13B model was trained with a learning rate of 1e-6 for 2 epochs. Throughout training, we set the maximum image resolution to 262144 pixels and used a batch size of 1 per GPU. Training was conducted on 8 A800 GPUs.
Following mainstream evaluation practices, when calculating evaluation metrics, a relative error of up to 14% is allowed for click and long press actions compared to the ground truth. For type actions, the input text must achieve a similarity of at least 80% with the ground truth text. All other actions must exactly match the ground truth to be considered correct.
The base OS agent models fine-tuned under this framework are based on Qwen2.5-VL-7B and Qwen2.5-VL-32B. The resulting agents are denoted as VeriOS-Agent-7B and VeriOS-Agent-32B.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Main Results</h3>

