<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>Base Model Selection Experiment</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main results, we observe that GUI-owl outperforms Qwen2.5-VL at the same parameter scale.
Nevertheless, the Qwen2.5-VL series is selected as our base model. It is therefore essential to validate whether this choice is justified.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To justify our base model selection, we conduct a base model selection experiment. As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 6.3. Base Model Selection Experiment ‣ 6. Analysis and Discussion ‣ VeriOS: Query-Driven Proactive Human-Agent-GUI Interaction for Trustworthy OS Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, GUI-owl’s foundational capabilities are not as strong as those of Qwen2.5-VL.
This is because GUI-owl is a specialized model for the OS agent domain, while Qwen2.5-VL is a generalized model.
The judgment of scenario types and responses to human queries in OS agent tasks require a certain level of general language understanding, making Qwen2.5-VL a more effective choice as the base model.</p>
</div>
